---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const fickCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'x/d', label: 'rate at distance d', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: 'x/(2*d)', label: 'rate at double the distance', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'd', label: 'diffusion distance', min: 0.5, max: 3, step: 0.5, value: 1, unit: 'µm' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'concentration difference (arb. units)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 12, label: 'rate of diffusion' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const labCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'A', label: 'membrane surface area', min: 1, max: 10, step: 1, value: 4, unit: 'arb. units' },
    { name: 'g', label: 'concentration gradient', min: 1, max: 10, step: 1, value: 5, unit: 'arb. units' },
    { name: 'd', label: 'diffusion distance', min: 0.5, max: 5, step: 0.5, value: 1, unit: 'arb. units' },
    { name: 'T', label: 'temperature', min: 273, max: 323, step: 5, value: 293, unit: 'K' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'net rate (arb. units)', expr: 'A*g/d', unit: '' },
    { label: 'relative diffusion coefficient (D ∝ T)', expr: 'T/293', unit: '' },
    { label: 'relative time to cross (∝ d²)', expr: 'd*d', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const osmosisCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'pure water: water potential Ψ = 0', note: 'Pure water is the reference — the highest water potential there is.' },
    { math: 'dissolve solute → Ψ goes negative', note: 'More solute means a more concentrated solution and a lower (more negative) water potential.' },
    { math: 'water moves: high Ψ → low Ψ', note: 'Osmosis is the net movement of water molecules from a dilute solution (higher water potential) to a concentrated one (lower water potential) through a partially permeable membrane.' },
    { math: 'plant cell in dilute solution → turgid', note: 'Water enters; the vacuole swells and the cytoplasm presses outward on the cell wall. That turgor pressure is what keeps soft plant tissue firm.' },
    { math: 'plant cell in concentrated solution → plasmolysis', note: 'Water leaves; the vacuole shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the wall. The flaccid, plasmolysed cell loses support — a wilting lettuce, not a crisp one.' },
  ],
};

const vecCfg = { type: 'vector', unit: 'µm', x: { min: 0, max: 10 }, y: { min: 0, max: 10 }, start: [5, 2] };
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/movement-into-and-out-of-cells">
  <h2>The idea: diffusion</h2>
  <p>
    Every living cell is wrapped in a membrane a few nanometres thick, and everything the cell
    needs — oxygen, glucose, ions, water — has to cross it. The simplest crossing is
    <strong>diffusion</strong>: the net movement of particles from a region of their higher
    concentration to a region of their lower concentration, down a concentration gradient.
    No engine drives it. The energy is the <strong>kinetic energy</strong> of the molecules
    themselves, jostling randomly; each one zigzags without purpose, but where there are more
    of them on one side, more random steps cross that way than back. Net flow, zero design.
  </p>
  <p>
    The syllabus (0610 unit 3.1, Core) singles out four factors you should be able to
    investigate: <strong>surface area</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong>,
    <strong>concentration gradient</strong> and <strong>distance</strong>. The graph below shows
    the last two working together — rate climbs with the gradient but falls as the
    diffusion distance grows:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="doubling the diffusion distance halves the rate, at every gradient" widget="plotter" config={fickCfg} />

  <p>
    In real membranes all four factors trade off, which is exactly why your alveoli are
    one cell thin and why a flatworm stays flat. Move the sliders — notice how brutal distance
    is: crossing time grows with the <em>square</em> of the distance, so ten times further
    away is a hundred times slower:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="surface area × gradient ÷ distance — the anatomy of every exchange surface" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />

  <p>
    Why does the distance factor rule biology? Because a simple body plan can live on
    diffusion alone — flatworms are flat precisely so no cell sits more than a fraction of a
    millimetre from the outside — but anything thicker needs transport systems and exchange
    surfaces. That is the single most important idea in this topic: <strong>diffusion of gases
    and solutes matters to every organism</strong> (Core point 4), and every organ built for
    exchange — alveoli, gills, leaves, root hairs — is a variation on the same four-factor
    theme: maximise area and gradient, minimise distance, keep the medium moving.
  </p>

  <h2>Osmosis and water potential</h2>
  <p>
    Water is the solvent of life: digestion happens in solution, excretion carries waste
    dissolved in water, and blood and sap transport everything dissolved (unit 3.2, Core).
    Because water molecules are tiny, they slip through the membrane's
    <strong>partially permeable</strong> structure by osmosis — and the direction of that
    movement is set by <strong>water potential</strong>, a Supplement idea worth learning
    properly because it explains turgor, plasmolysis and water uptake in five honest steps:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="water potential, from definition to wilting lettuce" widget="equationStep" config={osmosisCfg} />

  <p>
    Osmosis can be measured with dialysis tubing (an artificial partially permeable membrane):
    fill it with strong sugar solution, weigh it, and watch the mass climb as water enters.
    Immerse potato strips in solutions of different concentrations instead, and length changes
    map the point where the tissue neither gains nor loses water.
  </p>

  <h2>Active transport: pumping uphill</h2>
  <p>
    Diffusion only runs downhill. But root hairs mineral ions from soil where the soil is
    <em>diluter</em> than the cell sap — the wrong way for diffusion — and your gut absorbs
    glucose even when the gut contents are weaker than the blood. For that, membranes use
    <strong>active transport</strong> (unit 3.3): movement from lower to higher concentration,
    <strong>against</strong> the gradient, powered by energy from respiration. Supplement
    point 3 names the machinery: <strong>protein carriers</strong> in the membrane pick up the
    molecule or ion, change shape using ATP, and release it on the far side.
  </p>
  <p>
    Diffusion is random, but its outcome is still a displacement. Watch one molecule's
    <strong>net</strong> journey — thousands of random zigzags collapse into one arrow whose
    components are exactly the Supplement-worthy point that net movement, not the
    individual path, is what diffusion delivers:
  </p>

  <Widget no="4" caption="a diffusing molecule's net displacement — x crosses the membrane, y drifts along it; drag the head" widget="diagramBuilder" config={vecCfg} />

  <p>
    The price of pumping uphill is paid in ATP, which makes active transport a direct
    tax on respiration: poison the mitochondria and the pumps stop within minutes, while
    diffusion carries on regardless — it never needed the energy budget. That dependence is
    also how the process is demonstrated experimentally: keep a root in conditions where
    respiration is slowed (cold, or oxygen-starved) and ion uptake falls with it, even though
    the concentration gradient has not changed. Wherever a cell accumulates a substance
    against the gradient — mineral ions in root hairs, glucose in the gut, urea in the kidney
    tubule — expect to find mitochondria crowded around those membranes, fuelling the carriers.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 3; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 5 (Structure and Function of Plasma Membranes); Campbell Biology (Urry et al.), 12th ed., ch. 7–8.</p>
</TopicPage>
